diff --git a/specs/hal-engine-tool-input-validation/spec.md b/specs/hal-engine-tool-input-validation/spec.md
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..36e620d
--- /dev/null
+++ b/specs/hal-engine-tool-input-validation/spec.md
@@ -0,0 +1,26 @@
+# Tool Input Validation
+
+| Field  | Value                |
+| ------ | -------------------- |
+| Issue  | re-cinq/HALEngine#37 |
+| Status | In Progress          |
+
+Every tool registered in `src/orchestration/tools/registry.ts` declares a JSON Schema `inputSchema` that is forwarded to the configured AI provider, and `ToolRegistry.execute` validates the model-supplied `input` against that schema before calling the tool's executor. When validation fails, `execute` returns a rejection `ToolResponse` describing what was wrong rather than throwing, so the orchestrator feeds the failure back to the model as a `tool_result` within the existing tool-round budget.
+
+## Validation at execute time
+
+- When `input` omits a property listed in `required`, `ToolRegistry.execute` returns a `ToolResponse` whose `result` names the complete missing-property path as required and never invokes the executor ([validated by: rejects input missing a required property before calling the executor](../../src/orchestration/tools/registry.test.ts#L5), [validated by: names the complete path for a missing nested required property](../../src/orchestration/tools/registry.test.ts#L145), [acceptance coverage](../../src/orchestration/tools/registry.acceptance.test.ts#L16)).
+- When `input` supplies a property whose value does not match its declared JSON Schema type, `ToolRegistry.execute` returns a `ToolResponse` naming the failing field path and expected type without invoking the executor, and neither `result` nor the emitted log record contains the offending property value; the same type enforcement applies when the property schema is a local JSON Schema reference ([validated by: reports the path and expected type for a rejected typed property](../../src/orchestration/tools/registry.test.ts#L30), [validated by: names the rejected property when Ajv reports a legacy dataPath](../../src/orchestration/tools/registry.test.ts#L46), [validated by: rejects a type mismatch declared through a local schema reference](../../src/orchestration/tools/registry.test.ts#L228), [acceptance coverage](../../src/orchestration/tools/registry.acceptance.test.ts#L46)).
+- When `input` satisfies `required`, declared property types and `enum`, `ToolRegistry.execute` invokes the executor once and passes the input object through unchanged — including undeclared extra properties — without injecting `additionalProperties: false`, coercing types or applying defaults. Constraints outside that contract, such as `minLength`, do not reject the call ([validated by: passes input that violates an out-of-contract schema constraint to the executor unchanged](../../src/orchestration/tools/registry.test.ts#L69)).
+- Declared `required`, type, and `enum` rules remain enforced when a schema composes them with `allOf` or `anyOf`; unrelated constraints remain outside the registry validation contract ([validated by: enforces declared input rules composed through allOf](../../src/orchestration/tools/registry.test.ts#L94), [validated by: enforces declared input rules composed through anyOf](../../src/orchestration/tools/registry.test.ts#L118)).
+- When `ToolRegistry.execute` is called with a tool name that has not been registered, it throws `Unknown tool: <name>` because an unregistered tool is a programming or configuration error rather than a recoverable input mistake.
+
+## Schema compilation and caching
+
+- A static `ToolDefinition` has its complete `inputSchema` compiled once at `register` time, so a schema that is not valid JSON Schema throws synchronously from `register` with an error message naming the offending tool ([validated by: rejects an invalid properties schema at register time and names the tool](../../src/orchestration/tools/registry.test.ts#L167), [acceptance coverage](../../src/orchestration/tools/registry.acceptance.test.ts#L94)).
+- A static tool's `inputSchema` is compiled at `register` time and reused on every `execute` call without re-reading or re-compiling `definition.inputSchema` ([validated by: compiles a static schema once and reuses it across executions](../../src/orchestration/tools/registry.test.ts#L182)).
+- A function-valued `ToolDefinitionSource` resolves its definition on each `execute` call and validates against the `inputSchema` returned for that call, caching compiled validators by schema object reference (`WeakMap`) so distinct returned schemas each enforce their own rules ([validated by: validates each call against the schema the definition function returns at that call](../../src/orchestration/tools/registry.test.ts#L201)).
+
+## Orchestrator tool-loop recovery
+
+- Because a schema rejection returns a `ToolResponse` instead of throwing, `src/orchestration/chatOrchestrator.ts` sends the rejection `result` back to the provider as a `tool_result` message, allowing the model to issue a corrected tool call on the next round and complete the turn within `maxToolRounds` ([validated by: returns a schema rejection as a tool_result in the orchestrator loop so the model can correct the call on the next round](../../src/orchestration/tools/registry.acceptance.test.ts#L112)).
